WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.510
TV, you break our hearts.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:09.890
We give you hours of our time, and you promise
us a level of consistent bliss, and then you

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.170
stab us right in the back.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:18.400
During the course of almost any great TV show,
you'll find at least one disastrously off-key

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.400
episode.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.400
Expect spoilers.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.650
Here are a few TV shows nearly ruined by one
episode.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.859
"The worst episode ever!"

00:00:27.859 --> 00:00:30.199
The Walking Dead - Thank You

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:34.719
The world’s most popular zombie show has
enough suspense and desperate relationship

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:41.430
dynamics to keep itself going indefinitely—which
is just one reason why the season six episode,

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.210
"Thank You," didn't need to happen like it
did.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:49.850
Glenn's fake-out-dumpster-death was the first
time the showrunners pulled a deceptive camera

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:50.940
trick.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.280
Even though viewers quickly saw through the
ruse, they were not quick to forgive.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.309
The Walking Dead changed from a straightforward
post-apocalyptic drama into a show that was

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:02.659
comfortable deceiving the audience.

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:07.600
Much to the disappointment of the audience,
similar camera tricks made a controversial

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.410
reappearance during the Season 6 finale.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.440
The Simpsons - Natural Born Kissers

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:20.780
Critics often debate the exact point when
the once-amazing Simpsons began to crumble,

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:26.180
though Slate, The New York Post, and plenty
of other cultural pundits all seem to agree

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:30.040
that it was "somewhere during the ninth and
tenth seasons."

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:35.940
So, it’s fair to choose the ninth season
finale as the definitive point where the show

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:41.930
was no longer the series we knew and loved,
since even the official Simpsons website admits

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.580
that the popularity of that season was sub-par.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.730
The off-key, overtly sexual theme of “Natural
Born Kissers” could be attributed to the

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:56.070
fact that it was writer Matt Selman's first
crack at the show, but quality control had

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.000
already shown signs of slipping.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.960
Viewers started tuning out.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:07.430
By the time Season 10 premiered, 1.6 million
viewers had already left, making it the lowest-viewed

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:10.410
Simpsons premiere until Season 25.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:16.800
"Ahhhh ha ha ha ha, oh man that's good!"

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.249
Xena: Warrior Princess - Lifeblood

00:02:20.249 --> 00:02:26.010
There are hundreds of Xena moments that test
the downward limits of good TV, but viewers

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:30.540
will never forget the laziness of season five's
"Lifeblood."

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:36.680
In an attempt to build an unstoppable trifecta
of ancient adventures geared to modern audiences,

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:42.870
Hercules and Xena producer Rob Tapert created
Amazon High, a two-hour pilot about a modern

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:46.999
girl transported to the time of the original
Amazons.

00:02:46.999 --> 00:02:52.400
The network wasn't into it, so Tapert cut
the footage down and crammed it into an episode

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.120
of Xena instead, adding ghosts and baby sacrifices.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.010
Despite featuring sci-fi royalty like Selma
Blair, Karl Urban, and Claudia Black, the

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.159
sliced-and-diced episode just confused viewers.

00:03:06.159 --> 00:03:11.639
Watching the episode was a labor that even
Hercules couldn't have handled.

00:03:11.639 --> 00:03:13.999
Doctor Who - Nightmare in Silver

00:03:13.999 --> 00:03:20.170
You can easily find a few thousand blog entries
with titles like "Why I Quit Doctor Who,"

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:25.659
or "The Doctor Is Out," and they all have
their own ideas of where Doctor Who jumped

00:03:25.659 --> 00:03:26.659
the shark.

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:30.139
Maybe the breaking point is the episode "Nightmare
in Silver."

00:03:30.139 --> 00:03:35.419
The Doctor brings some kids to a decommissioned
amusement park—but instead of using his

00:03:35.419 --> 00:03:40.310
time machine to bring them back to a time
when it wasn't a dangerous ruin crawling with

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.080
Cybermen, they hang out.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.779
Worst babysitter of all time.

00:03:45.779 --> 00:03:52.890
The entire episode is a series of unusually
huge plot holes which Radio Times called "execrable,"

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:59.629
"a flop," and "underwhelming,"...which are
polite British code words for "you've just

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:02.719
destroyed everything good in this world."

00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:05.680
Twin Peaks - The Man Behind the Glass

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:11.629
David Lynch is allowed a greater weirdness
quotient than almost any other director...

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:15.489
"It was crazy clown time!"

00:04:15.489 --> 00:04:22.160
...but "The Man Behind the Glass" during the
second season of the cult classic Twin Peaks

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.480
marks the spot where fans started to lose
their enchantment.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:33.870
For no discernable reason, resident bad boy
James decides to bust out a guitar and sing

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.130
a sleepy, ultra-falsetto ballad to a couple
of girls.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:47.100
"Just youuuuu and I."

00:04:47.101 --> 00:04:52.250
The ladies join him in an impromptu singalong,
along with other invisible instruments.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:58.470
It's the least believable part of a show that
actively incorporated, well, stuff like this...

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.040
Thanks for watching!

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.280
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:14.150
And leave us a comment to let us know which
TV episodes you almost think ruined your favorite

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:14.650
shows...

